mail&amp;ust=1793240862864000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1ABuD9MGhTEnDiNr_0HSaa\">Davies</a> \r\n                                        </td> \r\n                                    </tr> \r\n                                </table> \r\n                            </td> \r\n                        </tr> \r\n                        <tr> \r\n                            <td style=\"padding:0px;border-collapse:collapse;border-left:1px solid #ccc;border-right:1px solid #ccc;border-top:0;border-bottom:0;padding:0 15px 0 16px;background-color:#fff;padding-top:5px;padding-bottom:10px\"> \r\n                                <table cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" border=\"0\" width=\"100%\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:20px\"> \r\n                                    <tr> \r\n                                        <td style=\"padding:0px;border-collapse:collapse;padding:0 0 10px 0\"> \r\n                                            <p style=\"margin-top:0;margin-bottom:10px;margin:10px 0 0 0\">The Spark executor could use lots of memory to cache some data, then it&#39;s very expensive to fork a R process (or easy to fail).</p> \r\n                                            <p style=\"margin-top:0;margin-bottom:10px;margin:10px 0 0 0\">The solution is fork a lightweight daemon (in R) process in the beginning (when needed), call the daemon to launch R worker when needed.</p> \r\n                                            <p style=\"margin-top:0;margin-bottom:10px;margin:10px 0 0 0\">This is only works in Linux/Mac, In Windows, it will still fork a R worker from JVM.</p> \r\n                                        </td> \r\n                                    </tr> \r\n                                </table> \r\n                            </td> \r\n                        </tr> \r\n                        <tr> \r\n                            <td style=\"padding:0px;border-collapse:collapse;border-left:1px solid #ccc;border-ri